Hi [~accountid:6048a13603ceda0069a64c8f] according to information provided in this link [https://www.karys.lt/bendraukime/d.u.k./tarnybos-atidejimas/225|https://www.karys.lt/bendraukime/d.u.k./tarnybos-atidejimas/225|smart-link] with frequently asked questions:  Can an employer write a request for deferment of service?

Answer: In the context of the legislation on conscription, *applications for deferment of service by organisations are not considered.* A conscript may attach to his/her application documents from other natural or legal persons proving the factual circumstances. So conscripts themselves need to apply for deferment, not employer. 

I would suggest both employees that received letters from authorities to check wether some of the listed grounds for exemption or deferment is applicable to them and if yes, to apply for it. Listed grounds (translated from Lithuanian in DeepL):

# persons who have been declared incapacitated or have limited capacity;
# persons with disabilities (where the level of incapacity for work is determined to be up to 55 per cent) ;
# persons declared unfit for compulsory military service by the Military Medical Examination Board;
# soldiers in the ranks of the armed forces;
# persons who have not attained basic military readiness when they reach the age of 60;
# women, except for those who have expressed in writing their wish to become conscripts and/or have completed medical, nursing or midwifery studies at a higher education institution.
# pupils of general education and vocational training schools aged 21 years or over;
# students of higher education institutions - once for one period of study in a first cycle (vocational bachelor's and bachelor's degree) study programme or a programme of integral studies, in a postgraduate study programme or a programme of integral studies, in a non-degree programme of studies, in a postgraduate study programme, in a doctoral programme;
# members of the Seimas, the Government, the European Parliament and municipal councils;
# judges and prosecutors;
# members of the internal service system;
# officials of the Security Service of the Management of the Republic of Lithuania, the State Security Department system and the Special Investigation Service;
# suspects and accused in criminal proceedings or persons serving a sentence which prevents them from performing compulsory initial military service, as well as persons who have served a custodial sentence;　
# raising a minor child alone or if they have been granted leave to care for the child until the child reaches the age of three;
# when the spouse or common-law partner of a conscript is on maternity leave;
# conscripts with two or more children under the age of three years;
# pregnant women who have produced a medical certificate of pregnancy;
# appointed, in accordance with the procedure laid down by law, as the sole guardian of a disabled person or as the guardian of a person with limited legal capacity;
# members of Lithuanian religious communities or societies having the rights of a legal person, who, in accordance with the procedure established by the canons, statutes or other norms of these religious communities or societies, have been elected or appointed to carry out pastoral work in those religious communities or societies;
# conscripts who, following an examination of their state of health, have been found to be unable to perform compulsory initial military service on account of a temporary medical condition, until the end of their temporary medical condition;
# conscripts who, at the end of their conscription, remain uncontested for compulsory initial military service;
# conscripts who would suffer disproportionate damage to personal or public interests during their service, which could be avoided if the conscript were to perform his/her service at another time (in accordance with the procedure laid down by the Minister for National Defence).

Pursuant to Article 15(14) of the Law on Military Conscription of the Republic of Lithuania, a conscript who is called up for compulsory military service may apply to the regional unit of the Military Conscription and Recruitment Service for postponement of his/her service on the grounds of disproportionate damage to his/her personal or public interests,  the conscript considers that such damage would arise and be avoided if the conscript performed his/her compulsory military service at another time. Thus, in making such a request, *the conscript must prove two things: that the compulsory military service will cause disproportionate harm, and that the harm will be avoided if the service is performed at another time.* The assessment of the circumstances in which compulsory military service would cause disproportionate harm depends on the form of service performed. *For example, in the case of an application for a 9-month deferment of permanent compulsory initial military service, circumstances which could be considered sufficient for a deferment of service could include the raising of two or more young children, being the sole breadwinner in an able-bodied family, or being drafted during the pregnancy of his wife.* In contrast, in the case of an application for a deferment of 20 to 30 days of training as a member of the Ready Reserve, the circumstances for establishing disproportionate harm must be more serious, for example, not only must the conscript prove that he or she is raising young children, but also that the children would not be cared for during the Ready Reserve training.

A conscript who receives a decision of the Selection Board or other institution administering conscription and disagrees with the decision has the right to lodge an appeal in accordance with the procedure laid down by the Minister of National Defence, within 10 days from the date of receipt of the decision. The complaint shall be signed by the conscript or his authorised person, shall state the conscript's name, surname, date of birth, contact details (address, telephone number, e-mail address), the grounds and/or motives of the complaint, and the arguments in support thereof. The complaint must be accompanied by documents supporting the grounds and/or reasons for the complaint and a copy of the decision of the institution administering conscription in the national defence system. The complaint may be submitted to:
(1) in writing (when the conscript arrives at the Ministry of National Defence or sends it by registered mail), electronically (scanned or signed with an electronic signature) to the Ministry of National Defence e-mail address [pilieciuaptarnavimas@kam.lt|mailto:pilieciuaptarnavimas@kam.lt];
2) in writing through the regional unit of the Conscription and Recruitment Service which administers the conscript.
Complaints shall be examined by a permanent commission for examination of complaints of conscripts, the composition and regulations of which shall be approved by the Minister of National Defence.

